{# DNOS IS-IS.

   INSTANCE NAME: netlab's own, from isis.instance (default "Gandalf"), so unlike OSPF this
   template does not have to invent one. That matters on hardware: both lab boxes already run an
   operator instance called CORE, and netlab creating "Gandalf" beside it collides with nothing.
   OSPFv2 had to be given a name knob (see ospf/dnos.j2) precisely because netlab's instance was
   called "ospf" and merged into anything of that name.

   WHAT DNOS REQUIRES ACROSS IS-IS INSTANCES -- measured against a container carrying an
   operator-shaped CORE instance, because guessing it from the OSPF rules gets it backwards:

    * THE SYSTEM ID MUST BE THE SAME, not distinct:

          ERROR: System ID must be the same for all networks in all ISIS instances.

      The AREA may differ (49.0002... beside CORE's 49.0001... commits fine); only the system-id
      half of the NET is shared. This is the one rule netlab cannot satisfy by itself on a device
      it does not own, because the operator's system id is not in the netlab data model and
      netlab plans its own from the node id. Tell netlab the truth in the topology instead -- the
      same fix bgp/dnos.j2 documents for the loopback, and there is a worked example in
      labs/dnos-hw-isis/:

          nodes:
            RNS72XC:
              isis.net: 49.0001.1920.0000.5000.00     # the box's OWN system id

      Read it off the box with "show config protocols isis instance <theirs>". Without it the
      candidate is refused by "commit check", so nothing is applied and nothing is damaged -- but
      the default render cannot come up on a box that already runs IS-IS, and no template change
      can make it, because the value is only knowable from the box.

      Pinning a netlab ATTRIBUTE here, where ospf/dnos.j2 uses a netlab_dnos_* variable for its
      router-id, is deliberate rather than drift: isis.net is visible in the rendered config and
      in the topology under review, while a group_vars override is invisible to "netlab initial
      -o" (see the distance note below).

    * ADMINISTRATIVE DISTANCES MUST DIFFER, and DNOS compares the EFFECTIVE values, not just the
      configured ones -- two instances both sitting on the default 115 are refused:

          ERROR: Administrative-distances of ISIS protocols Gandalf and CORE cannot be equal.

      So a second instance needs one, and it is emitted for exactly the nodes that will be a
      second instance: those netlab does not own. A lab node has no operator instance to collide
      with and keeps the stock distance, so IS-IS route preference in a lab still matches every
      other netlab device.

      netlab_dnos_isis_distance overrides the 120 default. 120 is deliberately WORSE than the
      stock 115, so that where netlab's instance and the operator's both learn a prefix the
      operator's wins and netlab never quietly takes over a production path. If the operator is
      themselves on 120 the commit check refuses it and names both instances, so the clash is
      loud and the override is the answer -- but note that setting it in topology groups.*.vars
      will NOT show up in "netlab initial -o", which merges DEVICE group_vars only. Preview it
      from the candidate diff the deploy prints instead.

    * A ROUTED INTERFACE MAY BE IN ONLY ONE INSTANCE; A LOOPBACK MAY BE IN SEVERAL:

          ERROR: Interface 'ge100-0/0/1' can't be enabled on more than one IS-IS instance.

      while lo0 in both CORE and Gandalf commits cleanly. THIS IS NOT AN IS-IS PECULIARITY -- a
      second OSPF instance takes a loopback that another instance already has, too (verified with
      a matching router-id and distinct distances, so nothing else masked it). The rule for both
      protocols is the same: loopbacks are shareable, routed interfaces are not.

      That matters when choosing PORTS on a box netlab does not own. interface_name maps netlab
      link N onto ge10-0/0/N, and the 72XC's CORE instance already holds ge10-0/0/5 and
      ge10-0/0/20 (plus bundle-5.10, ge100-0/0/64.10, ge100-0/0/70.20 and lo0). A one-link
      topology lands on ge10-0/0/1 and is fine; a five-link topology reaches ge10-0/0/5 and is
      refused. Give netlab ports the operator's IS-IS does not already carry.

   IDENTITY: the system loopback is excluded unless netlab is the owner of record, exactly as in
   ospf/dnos.j2. On a device in service the loopback is the operator's -- it anchors their
   router-id and their prefix-SID -- so netlab does not advertise it out of an instance netlab
   created, even though DNOS would let it.

   TWO LEAVES ARE REJECTED ON A LOOPBACK, and netlab can be asked for both, so both guards below
   are load-bearing rather than defensive:

    * "passive". netlab marks loopbacks passive, and on a loopback the word does not exist:
      "The command 'passive enabled' failed, Reason: Unknown word." On a real interface it does
      exist but takes a value -- a bare "passive" there is an "Incomplete command", not an
      unknown one.
    * "level". modules/isis.yml declares attributes.loopback.type, so a topology may legitimately
      say "nodes.d1.loopback.isis.type: level-1" -- which renders "level level-1" under lo0 and
      fails the same way, taking the WHOLE load merge with it.

   "metric" under the loopback's address-family is accepted, so it needs no such guard.

   METRIC HANGS OFF THE ADDRESS FAMILY, not off the interface: "metric" directly under the
   interface is rejected as an unknown word, and under "address-family <af>-unicast" it commits.
   So a cost applies per address family, and is emitted once per family the interface runs. #}
{% set _own  = netlab_manage_identity|default(True) %}
{% set _lb   = loopback.ifname|default('') %}
{% set _if   = [] %}
{% for l in netlab_interfaces|default([])
     if l.isis is defined and (_own or l.ifname != _lb) %}
{%   set _ = _if.append(l) %}
{% endfor %}
protocols
  isis
    instance {{ isis.instance }}
{#  IS-IS has an instance-level admin-state; OSPF and BGP have none. Without it the instance is
    configured and does nothing. #}
      admin-state enabled
      iso-network {{ isis.net }}
      level {{ isis.type|default('level-2') }}
{% if not _own %}
      administrative-distance {{ netlab_dnos_isis_distance|default(120) }}
{% endif %}
{% for l in _if %}
{%   set _is_lb = l.type|default('') == 'loopback' %}
      interface {{ l.ifname }}
        admin-state enabled
{%   if l.isis.network_type|default('') == 'point-to-point' %}
        network-type point-to-point
{%   endif %}
{%   if l.isis.passive|default(False) and not _is_lb %}
        passive enabled
{%   endif %}
{#   Per-interface circuit type, suppressed on a loopback where DNOS has no such leaf (see
     above). Only emitted when the topology asked for one that differs from the instance level --
     an interface with no level of its own inherits the instance's. #}
{%   if l.isis.type is defined and l.isis.type != isis.type|default('level-2') and not _is_lb %}
        level {{ l.isis.type }}
{%   endif %}
{%   for af in ["ipv4","ipv6"] if isis.af[af]|default(False) and l[af] is defined %}
        address-family {{ af }}-unicast
{%     if l.isis.metric is defined or l.isis.cost is defined %}
          metric {{ l.isis.metric|default(l.isis.cost) }}
{%     endif %}
        !
{%   endfor %}
      !
{% endfor %}
    !
  !
!
